      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Crown Copyright © 2011. Published by Elsevier B.V. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Soluble forms of CD28 (sCD28) and CTLA-4 (sCTLA-4) were associated with many autoimmune diseases like Sjögren's syndrome, systemic lupus erythematosus, asthma, and autoimmune myasthenia gravis. However, sCD28 and sCTLA-4 in neuromyelitis optica (NMO) and multiple sclerosis (MS) patients were less studied."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To measure the plasma sCD28, sCTLA-4 in NMO and MS patients, and investigate whether sCD28 and sCTLA-4 possible use as sensitive biomarkers for diseases activity."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Plasma concentrations of sCD28, sCTLA-4 were measured by an enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) in NMO (n=22), MS (n=21) patients and controls (n=18)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "The concentration of sCD28 levels were higher in the inflammatory demyelinating diseases cohort compared with the controls (NMO, p=0.034; MS, p=0.026) and the levels of sCD28 were slightly higher in NMO compared with MS. The sCTLA-4 levels were lower in the MS subgroup compared with the controls (p=0.032). Both sCD28 and sCTLA-4 did not show any correlation with EDSS score in NMO and MS patients."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Our study revealed for the first time that the levels of increased plasma sCD28 and decreased plasma sCTLA-4 in NMO and MS patients, but had little correlation with clinical presentations."
          }
        ]
      },
